A||N||10||SmittyABP||Replacement Engine?||||1||
Z||000000||SmittyABP||04-24-2001||08:15 AM||||Hi..I have an 86 2.8 V6 SE and I was wondering, What other GM FWD 2.8 engines can i use? Please tell me what models/makes/years.....<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>SmittyABP<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8 V6<BR>www.zoominternet.net/~smitty1/fiero/||||reg||1
Z||000001||ironhedno3||04-24-2001||07:53 PM||||your best alternative other than a 3.4 is a 3.1 from a 91-93 lumina apv or pontiac transport..its a direct bolt in..other wise any front drive 2.8 with ironheads will get you by aslong as its 85 and newer||||reg||1
Z||000002||c m west||04-24-2001||08:04 PM|||| Here at the shop we are installing a 1993 Camaro 3.4 into a Fiero - all you have to do is drill on the other side of the block for the starter motor, then done deal<P>engine - Part # 12537104 ENG-L32G<BR> retails for $4071.90 .... We can get them for 1999.95<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dave Gunsul||04-24-2001||08:50 PM||||You also have to use a neutrally balanced<BR>flywheel/flexplate from an 88 V6 since the<BR>3.4 is internally balanced.<BR>The 85-87 Fiero V6 engines are externally<BR>balanced. The 3.4 injectors must also be used.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000004||ironhedno3||04-24-2001||09:01 PM||||if you have an automatic you have to grind one corner of the block down for 3.4 to work..im using all fiero stuff i.e injectors chip and regulator but 19lb injectors do make some differance..you can use your balancer since it is nuetral..but you will need an 88 flywheel for a stick..if you have an auto just knock the counterweight off<p>[This message has been edited by ironhedno3 (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000005||kanada||04-24-2001||09:14 PM||||well smitty, i've just went through what your asking about<P>after asking many, many, many different people from every possible walk of life who might know about interchanging engines, i can tell you the following:<P>you will hear that all the 2.8 engines out of that era will fit, but its not true<P>the fiero engine is a high output engine and this makes many things different, valves, cylinders, heads, etc...<P>the engine codes w and x and s etc... will not fit unless alot of things are changed over and even then i haven't been convinced that it is possible, even the starter is on the wrong side on some of these engines.  some people will try to tell you they will fit, but it isn't true, the only thing that fits are the mounting bolts.<P>your best bet is to look for an engine out of a fiero, i didn't think i would be able to find one, but without very much difficulty i did, and it was a great deal<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ironhedno3||04-24-2001||09:28 PM||||true the valve sizes are smaller..but not by much..it might even give better throttle response..as far as cams..they are so close its not even funny..other wise what i said before is true..trust me..ive built pretty much all 60 degree v6 engines||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||04-24-2001||11:44 PM||||The 'W' BLOCK is a direct fit. Everything else needs to be changed over to work. Heads, intake, front cover, pistons (if not flat) ect. The 'X' & 'S' starters are on the wrong side.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JR||04-25-2001||09:32 PM||||So Eric of West Coast Fiero:<P>Does that price included the newly drilled holes for the starter motor?<P>------------------<BR>85GT (daily driver 120,000 miles)<BR>88GT (highly modified sunday driver)||||reg||1
